## WHO IS THERE?
"Technology asks what is it for? and turns whatever it touches into a resource. Magic, here, asks who is there?  and seeks contact."

![sacred](/img/SacredCorner.jpg)
![exit](/img/RitualisingTheOrdinary.jpg)

i am seeking to understand why i am drawn to the idea of magic. illusions and sleight-of-hand are completely omitted for me in my definition of magic, but that didn't really bring me closer to what exactly i am describing when i think of magic.
Who is there? is a close beginning. magic is about building a relationship, it's a way of thinking about the world. The first section of the encounterology exhibit draws upon spiritual traditions of various cultures to cultivate a relationship with the protective forces in the home. The Romans called them Lares and Penates, and fed them and cultivated their goodwill. Magic has a give and a take, it is an encounter. In a sense, magic cannot ever come from a place of dominance. Ask who is there, and then listen.

![extinguisher](/img/RitualisingTechnology.jpg)

## WE LIVE IN A WORLD OF MANY WORLDS
"To some, a mountain is a deposit of ore. To others, it is a spirit that acts. In the first case you measure it, you cut into it, you mine it; in the second you greet them, you fear them, you petition them. These are not two opinions about the same mountain. The rock exists either way, but what it is for those who live with it differs entirely: on one side available matter, on the other an entity to be encountered. Two worlds, then, each with its own gestures, tools, stories, and protocols."

This idea put a lot of things into place in my mind. As a Canadian now in Europe, i think often about indigeneity. Canada is a country of many narratives, stories, and ways of approaching the world, and increasing visibility for indigenous traditions. the complications of which i am writing out of this note, though will come up for me in other ways. This notion helped me rethink the impacts of colonialism in my home, the idea that westerners in canada brought with them a different world than the one that they were discovering. maybe there could have never been true understanding 
